scene wardrobe

// ═════════════ WARDROBE — dressing a character from the script ═════════════
// The wardrobe is three parts, and only ONE of them is script:
//
//   1. CATALOG — which outfits exist, what they cost, which story variable each
//      slot drives.  Lives in manifest.json → sprites.<id>.wardrobe (the file
//      next to this one). It CANNOT be written in .lvns, like the whole cast.
//   2. MOMENT  — when the sheet opens:  wardrobe_show char="<entity id>"
//      ← that is this file, and it is the only line of script the mechanic needs.
//   3. RESULT  — what the player picked: an ordinary story variable, named by the
//      slot's "var" field. The sheet writes it back when the player confirms.
//
// wardrobe_show belongs to the novel-shell package (com.lvn.engine.shell). A host
// that installed only com.lvn.engine ignores it SILENTLY — the sheet never opens,
// the variable keeps whatever the script last set, and the scene plays on. So the
// beat must still read like a story if the sheet never appeared.

actor_map Mira=mira Dorn=dorn

// ─── 1. Seed the wardrobe variables ──────────────────────────────────────────
// The manifest declares  "var": "look"  on Mira's outfit slot, so `look` is what
// the sheet writes into. Seeding it does two jobs: it is the outfit before the
// player touches anything, AND the outfit forever on a host without the shell.
look = "casual"
dorn_look = "dress"

bg /content/bg/dressing_room.jpg

// ─── 2. Wear the variable ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// An axis value in {braces} is a TEMPLATE — it is re-read from the variable every
// time the actor is drawn, including live while the player is trying things on.
// A literal value (outfit="armor", see the finale) is a costume the STORY forces:
// a try-on cannot override it.
// `hair` is deliberately NOT mentioned here: an axis the script leaves unset is
// filled by whatever the player has equipped, and by the manifest default before
// that. That is the difference between a slot WITH a "var" (story-visible) and a
// slot without one (wardrobe-only).
actor mira center outfit="{look}" emotion=neutral
Mira: The gala is in an hour and I own exactly three things.

// ─── 3. The moment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The sheet has no preview pane: the LIVE actor on stage is the mirror. Put the
// character on stage first — the shell will stage her if you forget, but then she
// walks in mid-beat instead of being there already.
wardrobe_show char="mira"

// ─── 4. The result ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `look` now holds the confirmed axis value; branch on it like any variable.
// A numeric-looking value ("3") is written back as a NUMBER, anything else as a
// string — compare the way the catalog spells it.
actor mira center outfit="{look}" emotion=smile
if look == "gown" -> gala
if look == "armor" -> guard
-> plain

:gala
Mira [smile]: Then the gown it is. Twenty crystals of very good judgement.
-> fitting_dorn

:guard
Mira [neutral]: Plate, then. The canapés can wait.
-> fitting_dorn

:plain
Mira [neutral]: The coat has survived worse parties than this one.
-> fitting_dorn

// ─── 5. A SECOND character ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `char` is just a cast entity id: anything with a wardrobe block can be dressed.
// This is the one thing the articy importer cannot express — its automatic
// substitution only ever opens the sheet for the protagonist — so an NPC fitting
// is authored by hand, exactly like this.
:fitting_dorn
actor dorn right emotion=neutral
Dorn: Do I have to wear the plumed one?
wardrobe_show char="dorn"
if dorn_look == "plume" -> teased
-> finale

:teased
Mira [smile]: You have to wear the plumed one.
-> finale

// ─── 6. A costume the story forces ───────────────────────────────────────────
// A literal axis value outranks both the variable and the player's try-on, so a
// plot outfit can never be undressed by accident. Use it for "she is in uniform
// because she is on duty", never for "she likes this one".
:finale
actor mira center outfit="armor" emotion=neutral
Mira: ...and then the guard turned out, and nobody was dressed for a party.
ENDING: «Dressed for the occasion»
-> __end
